\fs28 \cf6 \cb3 \strokec6 Vaibhav got his BS/MS in Computer Science from Rutgers in 2014. He was a USACS officer from 2010\'962012, and now works as a Software Engineer at Etsy.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 I spent 5 years in the Computer Science BS-MS program. I was a peer mentor for CS111 for 3 years.\cb1 \
\cb3 I\'92ve met a wide range of students in the CS department. I\'92ve helped students with assignments, and explained concepts to them over and over again. This blog post explains the patterns for success and failure that I have noticed.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 Your performance in intro courses is incredibly important. Develop a good mental model of programming early in your coursework.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 A good performance in the intro courses builds the foundation for your CS knowledge. Without it, you are as useful as a mathematician that cannot add fractions, or solve linear equations.\cb1 \
\cb3 By the time you get through CS111, you should have a clear mental model of how problem solving is accomplished through code. It\'92s easy to get distracted as a 111 student by the complexities of Java, Eclipse, jEdit, your weekly assignments, and course project. This is especially true if you come to 111 with no programming experience. It might feel like all you\'92re doing is staying afloat week after week, assignment after assignment, milestone after milestone. Someone tells you that using Eclipse is good/bad. Someone tells you that you should put curly braces on the same line as a function, as opposed to putting it on its own line. It can be very overwhelming.\cb1 \
\cb3 If at the end of 111, you are able to take a problem given to you, think about how it\'92s solved, write code to solve it, and debug your code until it works, you have done well. This seems like a hard thing to measure, so I\'92m going to give you a couple of litmus tests to gauge your performance.\cb1 \
\cb3 Consider the problem below:\cb1 \
\cb3 Write a program that prints the numbers from 1 to 100. But for multiples of three print\'a0
\f3\fs30 \cb5 Lemon
\f2\fs40 \cb3 \'a0instead of the number and for the multiples of five print\'a0
\f3\fs30 \cb5 Juice
\f2\fs40 \cb3 . For numbers which are multiples of both three and five print\'a0
\f3\fs30 \cb5 LemonJuice
\f2\fs40 \cb3 .\cb1 \
\cb3 Here is what the output of the program looks like from 1 to 20.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f3\fs32 \cf2 \cb5 1\cb1 \
\cb5 2\cb1 \
\cb5 Lemon\cb1 \
\cb5 4\cb1 \
\cb5 Juice\cb1 \
\cb5 Lemon\cb1 \
\cb5 7\cb1 \
\cb5 8\cb1 \
\cb5 Lemon\cb1 \
\cb5 Juice\cb1 \
\cb5 11\cb1 \
\cb5 Lemon\cb1 \
\cb5 13\cb1 \
\cb5 14\cb1 \
\cb5 LemonJuice\cb1 \
\cb5 16\cb1 \
\cb5 17\cb1 \
\cb5 Lemon\cb1 \
\cb5 19\cb1 \
\cb5 Juice\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 This problem should be a breeze for you after 111. If you cannot write code to solve this in 30 minutes, without someone\'92s help, you are in trouble.\cb1 \
\cb3 Here is another litmus test. If you can solve the first 5 problems on\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://projecteuler.net/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Project Euler}}\'a0without any help, you have learned problem solving. You can start worrying about other things. If you can\'92t do these problems, you need to spend more time learning to problem solve, writing code, and debugging code. You will not get very far in a CS degree before you do this.\cb1 \
\cb3 You can write code to solve a Project Euler problem, and submit the solution by creating an account. This is also a great way to learn a new programming language (This is how I learned the basics of Python).\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 Data Structures is incredibly important.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 Data Structures develops on your understanding of problem solving from 111 and teaches you about clever ways in which programmers manipulate data, and algorithms.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You need to know Data Structures to write C code (CS211 \'97 Computer Architecture, and CS214 \'97 Systems Programming).\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You need to know Data Structures to do well in Algorithms (CS344).\cb1 \
\ls1\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You need to know Data Structures to get through a tech interview.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 You can ask any upperclassman what they think the most important CS course is, and Data Structures will almost certainly be one of their top 3.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 The Story of 50% of the students in CS112\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf2 \cb3 Now, let me tell you the story of Alice. Alice is a metaphorical student that represents 50% of the CS112 roster.\cb1 \
\cb3 Alice took CS111, did fairly well in it (got better than a B). In 111, Alice often found it hard to listen to the professor in lecture. Every once in a while, she zoned out, and couldn\'92t figure out what Tjang or Sesh was saying. She went home, looked at the weekly assignment, and finished it (perhaps with some help from a friend, TA, or someone at the iLabs). Since every concept in lecture was reinforced in a weekly assignment, she understood most of the material that was covered in 111.\cb1 \
\cb3 When Alice took 112, she followed a similar path. Tjang/Sesh was lecturing about Heaps, Linked Lists, Graphs, Tail Recursion, Efficiency Analysis of Insertion Sort etc. She went on Facebook on her laptop/got a text from a roommate, subequently zoned out in class, and quickly lost track of what the professor was saying. Alice went home, there was a project due in two weeks. Alice worked very hard on the project. She definitely had some issues along the way, but she was able to ask Sesh/TAs/peers/people at the iLabs for help and get it done. She got an 85+ on all the 5 projects in CS112. Alice got around a 50 on both Data Structures exams, ended Data Structures with a C/C+, and moved on.\cb1 \
\cb3 Alice is the canonical example of a student that does poorly in the Rutgers CS degree, and here\'92s why.\cb1 \
\cb3 In 111, Alice had projects every week that forced her to learn material. The only thing in 112 that forces you to learn material is the exam, and that happens twice a semester. Sometimes, the first 112 exam is so early in the semester that there\'92s very little material covered on it. This means that the final covers a ton of material, and because Alice has been zoning out of 112 lecture on a regular basis, she does not know why Heapify is O(n), or why you would want to use a min heap as a frontier when implmenting Dijkstra\'92s shortest path algorithm.\cb1 \
\cb3 In order to avoid Alice\'92s pitfalls, you need to\'a0
\f4\i make sure that you understand every concept in Data Structures
\f2\i0 . Practically every sentence that Sesh or Tjang says in 112 is important. I understand that you are human, and cannot keep perfect concentration in an 80 minute lecture. But you need to be proactive about understanding all the concepts that are covered. This means that if you zoned out in the lecture about AVL Trees, you still need to make sure you learn it. You can go read the textbook after class. You can look up the material online. There\'92s some very good online courses that cover data structures. I strongly recommend the\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kMzH3tfP6f8&list=PLFE6E58F856038C69"}}{\fldrslt \ul lectures from Stanford\'92s Programming Abstractions course}}. You can ask upperclassmen to explain the concept to you.\cb1 \
\cb3 You need to do this before the day of your data structures exam, because the course covers\'a0
\f4\i a lot of important material
\f2\i0 , and you cannot learn all of it in a day.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 Pick classes carefully, especially after the intro sequence.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 1. Pay close attention to the professor that\'92s teaching the class.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f2\b0 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Don\'92t take a class just because the professor is easy. By doing this, you are learning nothing, and wasting both time and tuition dollars.\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Understand that a lot of the professors teaching you haven\'92t written a line of code in 15+ years. They cannot and will not teach you web or mobile app development. If they attempt to teach you this stuff, their material will likely be outdated and poorly researched.\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The theoretical classes are challenging and will require mental discipline. I highly recommend everything being taught by\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cs.rutgers.edu/~farach"}}{\fldrslt \ul Martin Farach-Colton}},\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cs.rutgers.edu/~steiger/"}}{\fldrslt \ul William Steiger}}, or\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cs.rutgers.edu/~allender"}}{\fldrslt \ul Eric Allender}}. These were my favorite professors. Their classes were engaging, memorable, and had challenging material.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 2. Prerequisites don\'92t matter\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f2\b0 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If you\'92ve done well in 111, 112, 211 and 205, you can take\'a0
\f5\b any
\f2\b0 \'a0other class in the department \'97\'a0
\f4\i even a graduate class
\f2\i0 .\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Do you see\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cs.rutgers.edu/~uli"}}{\fldrslt \ul Uli Kremer}}\'a0(an excellent compilers professor) teaching next semester? Take his class even though you haven\'92t taken 314.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 But V! I\'92m not smart. Everyone in this class is going to have an edge on me. I\'92m going to get a bad grade, lose my scholarship, and end up on the streets. Here\'92s what I have to say to this \'97 50% of the people taking a class don\'92t learn a thing. At the end of CS112, I want you to ask 10 of your classmates if they can implement a hash table. At the beginning of 211 and 214, I want you to look around you as they struggle to implement one in C. They\'92re not learning in 112, and they\'92re not learning in the prerequisite courses that you want to skip. They will have no edge over you.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 You will feel more challenged in a course if you take it without a prerequisite, but as long as you\'92re proactive about brushing up on material you don\'92t know, you\'92ll do just fine.\cb1 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Logistics \'97 you can\'92t register for a course on webreg if you haven\'92t taken the prereqs. Send the prof an email to get a prereq override \'97 tell them you\'92re interested in the material, and you\'92ll put extra time and learn stuff you don\'92t know.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf2 \cb3 3. Stop focusing on grades. Focus on concepts you\'92re learning\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\b0 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The classes I loved the most were the ones in which I was engaged by the professor\'92s lectures and assignments.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I took Operating Systems with a smart but boring professor. I was not engaged in lecture, but his assignments, exams were easy so I got an A. I regret wasting my time, because my grade did not reflect my knowledge of OS.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 I have friends that took OS with\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cs.rutgers.edu/~ricardob/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Ricardo Bianchini}}. Their assignments and exams were much harder, and they spent much more time on coursework. Most of them did not get As \'97 but they learned far more about OS than I did.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If your goal is to learn OS, don\'92t waste your time in a course that doesn\'92t teach you OS.\cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 If your goal is to get a piece of paper with your name on it, and \'93Bachelors in Computer Science\'94 under it, this advice isn\'92t for you. I loved learning about the hard problems in computer science from incredibly smart people that were experts in these fields.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 If you are not branching out from the material you learn in class, you will have a hard time getting hired as a developer.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 Your professors haven\'92t written code in 15+ years. They\'92re not going to teach you how to develop software \'97 don\'92t waste your time taking CS431.\cb1 \
\cb3 Start writing code in your free time to solve\'a0
\f5\b any problem
\f2\b0 \'a0that you have. I wrote\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://oss.rutgers.edu/rubus/"}}{\fldrslt \ul RUBUS}}\'a0when I was in 112. I used to maintain\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://eden.rutgers.edu/~vverna/"}}{\fldrslt \ul static web pages}}\'a0for a board game my roommate played with his high school friends. Everyone that\'92s good at programming has put in lots of time into it, and you will need to do the same.\cb1 \
\cb3 Talk to upperclassmen about ideas you have, and the things that they\'92ve built. Hang out in the CAVE, attend USACS events, and go to hackathons. All of this experience will add up, and help you land your first paid programming gig.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 Get paid to write code as early as possible.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 When I started taking CS classes, I didn\'92t understand why people got paid to sit at a computer and write for-loops. I thought the mysterious, legendary developers getting internships and part time jobs must\'a0
\f5\b know so much more
\f2\b0 \'a0about the web/mobile apps/algorithms. This is a classic case of impostor syndrome, and it creates a mental roadblock until you get paid to write code.\cb1 \
\cb3 Use your summers for internships or part time work ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.joelonsoftware.com/articles/CollegeAdvice.html"}}{\fldrslt \ul not for folding clothes}}). For your first development job, I recommend\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://oss.rutgers.edu/"}}{\fldrslt \ul Student System Administration at OSS}},\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://lcsr.rutgers.edu/"}}{\fldrslt \ul System Administration at LCSR (under Doug Motto)}},\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://toomuchmedia.com/"}}{\fldrslt \ul an internship at Too Much Media}}, or\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://hackny.org/"}}{\fldrslt \ul HackNY if you are lucky}}. You\'92ll want to do a more traditional tech internship at companies like Microsoft, Google or Etsy, but you\'92ll have an easier time getting these once you have some experience. If you hang around CS folks, you\'92ll hear about such opportunities frequently. Apply early and often \'97 you\'92ll have an easier time in October than in April.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb3 Participate in the community\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs40 \AppleTypeServices \cf2 \cb3 The Rutgers CS community, while not perfect,\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://medium.com/@maltzj/from-0-350-in-three-years-991501eba5c7"}}{\fldrslt \ul has grown a great deal over the last few years}}. You\'92ve got access to events like HackRU, HackNY, PennApps, and HackTCNJ. You\'92ve got access to the CAVE, the Hackerspace, and the Makerspace. USACS, RuMAD and the Rutgers Hackathon Club are active, and full of smart people. People taking the same classes as you have gone on to\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1LQDJuN2G8JKGIeqiQPtRVQochu9uyGcCt_SrTHEtGnc/edit#gid=0"}}{\fldrslt \ul amazing jobs}},\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://comminfo.rutgers.edu/news/iti-alum-transforms-from-self-taught-hacker-to-co-founder-of-the-hacker-league.html"}}{\fldrslt \ul start companies}}, and\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://techcrunch.com/2013/12/03/hacker-league-mashery-intel/"}}{\fldrslt \ul sell companies}}. Start reading\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://news.ycombinator.com/"}}{\fldrslt \ul hacker news}}, and\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://reddit.com/r/programming"}}{\fldrslt \ul /r/programming}}.\cb1 \
\cb3 Don\'92t forget to give back to the community. Teach CS111 recitation. Join the USACS board, and help plan events. Hang out at the CAVE and help underclassmen understand difficult concepts. Help the noobs out at hackathons.\cb1 \
\cb3 Make friends out of your peers.\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://vverma.net/static/cs509_hw.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \ul Impossible looking homework assignments}}\'a0will become easier. You\'92ll spend a silly amount of time{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://github.com/stripe-ctf/stripe-ctf-2.0"}}{\fldrslt \ul working on a CTF challenge}}, or\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://sia.github.io/game/"}}{\fldrslt \ul writing a game}}. You\'92ll get\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://github.com/v"}}{\fldrslt \ul one letter}}\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://github.com/x"}}{\fldrslt \ul Github}}\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://github.com/k"}}{\fldrslt \ul usernames}}\'a0together. After college, they\'92ll help you find jobs and offer you their couches.\cb1 \
\cb3 Rutgers is a great place to study Computer Science, and I hope your time there will be as memorable as mine was.\cb1 \
